AU  - Griffiths, P. D.
TI  - Leading article Prophylaxis against CMV infection in transplant patients
PY  - 1997
AB  - Once a patient becomes immunocompromised following transplantation, the natural history of CMV infection is well documented. Foci of latent infection reactivate and fail to be controlled by the inadequate cell-mediated immune response. This process is frequently initiated in the donated solid organ if the donor is seropositive; although seropositive recipients have significantly reduced risks of CMV disease, recipient immunity is only partially protective. 1 The situation after bone marrow transplantation (BMT) is distinct; it is the seropositive recipient who is most at risk of CMV disease, 2 and there is evidence that seropositive donors may adoptively transfer some immunity into the recipient, thereby reducing CMV disease. 3 Prospective monitoring of transplant patients shows that clinically accessible samples such as throat swab and urine routinely contain CMV, 4 and the quantity of viral load in the urine correlates with CMV disease. 5 Patients excreting CMV from urine or throat have a raised risk of future CMV disease (relative risk approximately 2). 4,6 Ongoing CMV replication may be controlled at these local sites by host defences, including humoral immunity, macrophages and polymorphonuclear leucocytes, together with the improving profile of cell-mediated immunity in patients whose allograft has become established without rejection and whose dose of immunosuppres sive drugs can, therefore, be reduced. However, in patients in less favourable situations, local immune responses may be overwhelmed and CMV may spill over into the blood. The detection of CMV viraemia heralds a phase where CMV is disseminated to multiple target organs and so portends a poor prognosis, with a relative risk of 6‐10 for future CMV disease. 4,6 However, the detection of viraemia is not a guarantee of end-organ disease. Presumably, local immune responses in target organs may in some patients be sufficient to reduce CMV replication and so abort the disease process. Nevertheless, most (approximately 60%) viraemic patients subsequently develop CMV disease, which always has a high morbidity and may still be fatal, despite prompt treatment. The disease process is complex and distinct for each target organ, but common features are that CMV viral loads are high 5 and that CMV may trigger a variety of secondary pathological processes which cannot necessarily be reversed by treating the underlying virus infection. Indeed, in the specific case of the lung, there is strong evidence that the disease is caused by a cell-mediated immune response provoked by CMV, especially in the presence of graft- versus-host disease. 7 It follows that treatment of established disease caused by CMV frequently responds poorly to anti-CMV therapy; indeed, there is not a single placebo-controlled, double-blind, randomized trial which shows clinical benefit against established CMV disease for any drug with anti-CMV activity. 8 Given this background, what other options are available for reducing CMV disease? Prophylaxis literally means treatment before disease. The term ‘prophylaxis’ is restricted here to studies where a drug was administered from the time of transplant onwards. Controlled clinical trials of ‘prophylaxis’ have reported success in preventing CMV disease. 9‐15 However, other forms of ‘prophylaxis’ are also possible for CMV and a distinct terminology has evolved to describe them. Where patients are monitored prospectively by collection of surveillance samples, the various stages of CMV infection described above can be monitored and treatment tailored appropriately. For example, treatment following the detection of CMV from a peripheral site, such as urine or saliva, is termed suppression. Where treatment is initiated following detection of viraemia, this is termed pre-emptive therapy. 16 The same term is also used in the special case where BMT patients may have a routine bronchial lavage performed at day 35. 17 Where asymptomatic CMV lung infection is detected, treatment with ganciclovir is successful, presumably because the drug is being given before the immunopathological process in the lung has become established. Each of these approaches requires rapid virus diagnosis, and several techniques of appropriate sensitivity, specificity and positive predictive value are now widely available. 4,6,18‐22
